# Linux Shells & Shell Scripting Notes

## What is a Shell?
A **Shell** is a command-line interpreter that acts as an interface between the **user** and the **Linux Kernel**. It accepts commands from the user and executes them.

---

# Useful Commands

## Check Current Shell
```bash
echo $SHELL
```

## List Available Shells
```bash
cat /etc/shells
```

## Switch to Another Shell
```bash
zsh
```

## Change Default Shell
```bash
chsh -s /usr/bin/zsh
```

---

# Types of Linux Shells

## 1. Bash (Bourne Again Shell)

### Features
- Default shell in most Linux distributions.
- Excellent for shell scripting.
- Supports command history.
- Supports tab completion.
- Stable and widely used.

### Advantages
- Large community support.
- Extensive documentation.
- Best shell for automation.

---

## 2. Fish (Friendly Interactive Shell)

### Features
- Beginner-friendly.
- Auto-corrects commands.
- Built-in syntax highlighting.
- Supports themes and customization.
- Smart tab completion.

### Advantages
- Easy to use.
- Modern interface.
- Better user experience.

---

## 3. Zsh (Z Shell)

### Features
- Modern shell.
- Advanced tab completion.
- Auto-correction.
- Supports plugins.
- Highly customizable.

### Advantages
- Powerful scripting.
- Compatible with Bash scripts.
- Works well with Oh My Zsh.

---

# Bash vs Fish vs Zsh

| Feature | Bash | Fish | Zsh |
|----------|------|------|------|
| Default Shell | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ |
| Scripting | Excellent | Limited | Excellent |
| Tab Completion | Basic | Advanced | Advanced |
| Syntax Highlighting | Plugin Required | Built-in | Plugin Required |
| Customization | Basic | Good | Excellent |
| Beginner Friendly | Medium | Excellent | Good |

